<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:lang="en-GB">
  <book xml:id="mathematics-book">
    <title>Mathematics</title>
    <subtitle>Explore, understand, practise</subtitle>
    <frontmatter>
      <preface xml:id="welcome">
        <title>Welcome</title>
        <p>Look. Predict. Explore. Practise. Explain. Try each question before revealing its answer.</p>
        <p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/apps/index.html">Browse Maths Apps</url> to find every interactive investigation by topic.</p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>

    <part xml:id="part-unifying"><title>Unifying Strand</title>
      <xi:include href="./unifying-strand.ptx" />
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-number">
      <title>Number</title>
      <xi:include href="./sets-chapter.ptx" />
      <xi:include href="./sequences-chapter.ptx" />
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-complex"><title>Complex Numbers</title>
        <section xml:id="complex-intro"><title>Beyond the real numbers</title>
          <p>We define <m>i</m> by <m>i^2=-1</m>. A complex number has the form <m>a+bi</m>, where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are real.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-algebra"><title>Algebra</title>
      <xi:include href="./algebra-language.ptx" />
      <xi:include href="./expanding-brackets.ptx" />
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-factorisation"><title>Factorisation</title>
        <section xml:id="factorisation-intro"><title>Reversing expansion</title>
          <p>Factorisation writes an expression as a product. For example, <m>6x+9=3(2x+3)</m>.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-functions"><title>Functions</title>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-functions"><title>Functions and Graphs</title>
        <section xml:id="functions-intro"><title>Inputs and outputs</title>
          <p>A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-calculus"><title>Calculus</title>
        <section xml:id="calculus-intro"><title>Change and accumulation</title>
          <p>Calculus develops ways to study rates of change and accumulated quantities.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-geometry"><title>Geometry</title>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-geometry"><title>Shapes and Reasoning</title>
        <section xml:id="geometry-intro"><title>Seeing mathematical structure</title>
          <p>We describe geometric objects using definitions, diagrams and carefully justified reasoning.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-trigonometry"><title>Trigonometry</title>
      <xi:include href="./trigonometry-chapter.ptx" />
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-statistics"><title>Statistics</title>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-statistics"><title>Understanding Data</title>
        <section xml:id="statistics-intro"><title>Data and variation</title>
          <p>Statistics helps us collect, summarise and draw justified conclusions from data.</p>
        </section>
      </chapter>
    </part>

    <part xml:id="part-probability"><title>Probability</title>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-probability"><title>Events and Counting</title>
        <section xml:id="probability-fcp"><title>The Fundamental Counting Principle</title>
          <p>When the first choice has <m>m</m> possibilities and the second has <m>n</m> possibilities, every combination of choices gives <m>mn</m> possibilities.</p>
          <exercise xml:id="exercise-fcp">
            <statement><p>How many two-character codes can be formed from three choices for the first character and four for the second, if every pair is allowed?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>3\cdot4=12</m>.</p></answer>
          </exercise>
        </section>
      </chapter>
      <chapter xml:id="chapter-counting"><title>Systematic Counting</title>
              <section xml:id="combinatorics-intro"><title>Arrangements and selections</title>
                <p>Combinatorics studies how to count possible arrangements and selections.</p>
              </section>
            </chapter>
    </part>
    <part xml:id="part-specifications">
      <title>Specifications</title>
      <xi:include href="./specifications.ptx" />
    </part>

    <backmatter>
      <glossary xml:id="mathematics-glossary">
        <title>Mathematical Words</title>
        <headnote><p>Use these definitions while reading. On the web, linked terms open short explanations.</p></headnote>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-area"><title>area</title><p>The amount of surface inside a shape. It is measured in square units, such as <m>\mathrm{cm}^2</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-cardinality"><title>cardinality</title><p>The number of distinct elements in a set. For set <m>A</m>, we write <m>\#A</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-complement"><title>complement</title><p>The elements in the universal set that are not in the given set.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-element"><title>element</title><p>One object belonging to a set.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-height"><title>height</title><p>The perpendicular distance from a base to the opposite side or vertex. For a rectangle here, it is the vertical side length.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-intersection"><title>intersection</title><p>The elements common to both sets. Written <m>A\cap B</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-multiple"><title>multiple</title><p>A number of the form <m>kn</m> for an integer <m>n</m>. Zero is a multiple of any non-zero integer <m>k</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-perimeter"><title>perimeter</title><p>The distance all the way around a shape, measured in length units such as centimetres.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-set"><title>set</title><p>A well-defined collection of distinct objects. Order and repetition do not affect the set.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-union"><title>union</title><p>All elements belonging to at least one of two sets. Written <m>A\cup B</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-universal"><title>universal set</title><p>The set of all elements under discussion, often written <m>U</m>.</p></gi>
        <gi xml:id="glossary-width"><title>width</title><p>A side length measured across the rectangle.</p></gi>
      </glossary>
    </backmatter>
  </book>
</pretext>
